```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on router1's eth0 interface (193.10.11.1/24), which is in a DOWN/DOWN
state (no UP or LOWER_UP flags). The IP address is correctly configured in the
kernel, but the interface itself is not up, so the Linux kernel never installs a
connected route for 193.10.11.0/24. Without that connected route, FRR's BGP
daemon on router1 cannot open a TCP session to the peer at 193.10.11.2; the BGP
summary confirms the neighbor is stuck in the "Active" state (perpetually trying
to connect). As a result, router1 learns no BGP route to 200.1.1.0/24, and any
packet from pc1 (195.11.14.2) destined for pc2 (200.1.1.2) is immediately
rejected by router1 with an ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" — exactly what was
observed.

Router2 is innocent: its eth0 is UP, and its FRR entered BGP Graceful Restart
Helper mode when the peer vanished, temporarily holding the stale 195.11.14.0/24
BGP route for up to 360 seconds. Restarting router2 would accomplish nothing.

Root cause: router1's eth0 was brought down (likely by an `ip link set eth0 down`
command or a container/link fault), severing the only physical path between the
two ASes.

Corrective fix: On router1, run `ip link set eth0 up`. This will restore the
connected route, allow BGP to re-establish within seconds, and exchange prefixes
so that both routers have full reachability — no restart of router2 is needed.
=== END ===
```